TYPE CONVERSION:  Automatic, implicit process where the compiler transforms a smaller datatype into a larger one (compatible datatype)

TYPE CASTING:  Manual, explicit process where the programmer forces the compiler to convert a larger data type into a smaller one 
using a specific operator, which can also cause data loss.
DIFFERENT WAYS OF TYPE CASTING:
1. Widening Casting (Implicit) -> automatic by compiler
    byte -> short -> char -> int -> long -> float -> double
2. Narrowing Casting(Explicit) -> Manually performed by the programmer
    double -> float -> long -> int -> char -> short -> byte

DEMO PROGRAM: 

public class conversionandcasting 
{
    public static void main(String args[])
    {
//type conversion example(implicit/widening)
int intval = 105;
long lval = intval;
float fval = lval;
System.out.println("type conversion"+"\n");
System.out.println("original integer value: "+ intval+"\n");
System.out.println("converted to long type: "+ lval+"\n");
System.out.println("converted to float type: "+ fval+"\n");

//type casting example(explicit/narrowing)
double dval = 9.87;
int truncatedint = (int)dval;
int largeint = 130;
byte byteval = (byte)largeint;
System.out.println("type casting"+"\n");
System.out.println("original double value: "+ dval+"\n");
System.out.println("casted to int : "+ truncatedint+"\n");
System.out.println("original largeint value: "+ largeint+"\n");
  System.out.println("casted to byte type: "+ byteval+"\n"); 
 }
}

